WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
How can the same offspring genotypes make groups that look different?

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.340
Watch pink snapdragons send their allele copies into an empty grid.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:14.660
The copies will stay fixed while the phenotype rule changes around them.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.680
The screen will track inherited copies separately from the visible traits they help

5
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.680
produce.

6
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.740
An allele is a version of a gene.

7
00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.080
Each parent can pass its R allele or its W allele.

8
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.840
The grid tracks every pairing.

9
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.680
It does not decide what each pairing looks like.

10
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.040
Keeping those jobs separate is the key to reading the result.

11
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:46.013
Before the tiles land, predict the combinations.

12
00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:50.433
Which pairing repeats, and which pairings appear at the corners?

13
00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:54.113
Hold your answer while the tiles keep moving.

14
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:59.273
Look for repeated paths rather than guessing from the parents' pink color.

15
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.120
The tiles land as RR, RW, RW, and WW.

16
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.820
The mixed pairing appears twice.

17
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:18.420
This genotype pattern comes from allele separation, so changing the phenotype rule

18
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.680
cannot change the grid.

19
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.240
The parents' pink phenotype never fused their R and W allele copies.

20
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:29.783
Now place an incomplete-dominance lens over the grid.

21
00:01:31.183 --> 00:01:35.363
RR becomes red, WW becomes white, and RW becomes pink.

22
00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:41.583
Every genotype has its own phenotype here, so the phenotype pattern matches the

23
00:01:41.583 --> 00:01:47.603
genotype pattern. The color looks blended, but the inherited copies remain distinct

24
00:01:47.603 --> 00:01:48.863
inside each plant.

25
00:01:49.933 --> 00:01:53.873
Codominance needs a different gene, so switch to Shorthorn cattle.

26
00:01:55.153 --> 00:01:58.193
The same three pairings give red, roan and white coats.

27
00:01:59.573 --> 00:02:03.493
A roan RW calf grows red hairs and white hairs side by side.

28
00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:08.493
The mixed genotype shows both effects instead of an in-between color.

29
00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:11.613
The allele pairings still do not move.

30
00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:17.207
Alleles remain separate, and each genetic tool reveals a different scale.

31
00:02:18.627 --> 00:02:20.487
The grid reveals allele combinations.

32
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:25.187
A phenotype key reveals how each combination appears.

33
00:02:26.627 --> 00:02:29.887
Confusing those views makes pink look like permanent blending.

34
00:02:31.107 --> 00:02:33.327
The tool must match the question you are asking.

35
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.880
The same separation helps with blood type.

36
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.740
A population has three common ABO alleles, but each person carries a pair.

37
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.140
Type AB red blood cells display both A and B markers, a codominant phenotype.

38
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.840
Type O cells display neither marker, while type A or B displays its matching marker.

39
00:02:56.933 --> 00:02:58.773
So do not ask which allele won.

40
00:03:00.073 --> 00:03:04.173
Ask which alleles were inherited, then ask what that pair does to phenotype.

41
00:03:05.433 --> 00:03:07.273
The first answer comes from the cross.

42
00:03:08.593 --> 00:03:10.813
The second comes from the inheritance pattern.

43
00:03:11.613 --> 00:03:16.033
That order keeps inheritance separate from the way a trait is displayed.
